"""
Token Bucket Rate Limiter — stop a flood.

WHAT IT DOES: An API, a login endpoint, a disk writer — anything that must serve a
steady rate and reject the rest. The token bucket: tokens drip into a bucket at a
fixed rate; each request spends one; empty bucket → request denied. Steady service,
bursts absorbed up to the bucket size.

WHEN TO USE: You need to rate-limit access to a resource — API calls per second,
logins per minute, disk writes per second. The token bucket allows short bursts while
enforcing a steady long-term rate.

GUARANTEES: In steady state, the granted rate approximates tokens_per_sec. Over 2
seconds at 10 tokens/sec, ~20 ops are granted and >900 are denied.

NOT: A correct token bucket. There is NO burst capacity cap — tokens accumulate
without bound during idle. After 100s idle at rate=10, the next 200 requests are ALL
granted instantly (the limiter doesn't limit during exactly the spike it exists to
stop). A correct bucket caps accumulation at its burst size (fix #10). Also has
silent-success output (no receipts line).
"""
